    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "An apparatus was designed to measure oil-brine capillary pressure and electrical resistivity of rocks at different brine saturations at elevated temperatures up to 400F, The instrument was capable of measuring these rock properties in both increasing as well as decreasing temperature sequence. Using a refined mineral oil and 0.58% NaCl brine capillary pressures and resistivity indices of two sandstone and one limestone samples were measured at different brine saturations at temperature levels of 175, 250 and 300F at a base pressure of 325 psig. Data were obtained for both drainage and imbibition and for both increasing as wall as decreasing temperature sequences, Formation resistivity factor of three sandstone and one limestone samples were measured at different temperatures during both heating and cooling sequences.Formation resistivity factor of all samples increased with temperature and. showed apparent hysteresis between heating and cooling sequence runs. The rat'-; of change of formation resistivity factor with temperature appeared to be a function of the ion-exchangeable clay content and the internal surface area of samples. The change in pore constriction factor is believed to be the primary reason for the change in the true formation resistivity factor of samples with temperature. The resistivity index and the brine saturation showed linear relation on log-log plot. The value of saturation exponent during drainage and imbibition were the same only up to a certain temperature for each sample. The saturation exponent in drainage increased and that during imbibition decreased for the sand stone samples, The drainage saturation exponent for the limestone sample did not show temperature sensitivity, The resistivity behavior appears to be a function of both pore-constriction and pore-tortuosity factors. The observation of previous investigators that the hysteresis between drainage and inhibition capillary pressure curves decreases with temperature has been confirmed. The irreducible water saturations obtained in decreasing temperature sequence runs appeared to be higher than those during increasing temperature sequence runs.",
